// tedititem.nv — the load-bearing interface: a piece of a document is
// either bytes that were already there or bytes this editor made, and
// the type says which.
//
// Every body here is a `todo()`; the signatures are the design.
//
// THE ONE IDEA.  `TeditRaw` has two variants and no third:
//
//     TeditSpan(start, end)   bytes [start, end) of the original text
//     TeditMade(text)         bytes this editor produced
//
// Every key, every value, every run of whitespace and every comment in
// a parsed document is one of the two.  Rendering is therefore a
// concatenation of raws in order, and the property the whole package
// exists for —
//
//     a byte the caller did not edit comes back identical
//
// is STRUCTURAL rather than promised.  It cannot be broken by an
// implementation that reflows a line or normalises a quote, because
// doing either would mean replacing a `TeditSpan` with a `TeditMade`,
// and `origin_of` reports that to the caller who asked.  A test can
// assert the property directly — `teditdoc.made_count(d) == 1` after
// one `insert` — instead of comparing two files and hoping the diff
// noticed.
//
// WHY NOT KEEP THE WHOLE TEXT AND PATCH SPANS.  That is the other
// design, and it is the one toml-nv's `tomledit` takes: hold the
// source, rewrite the span a `set` names, re-parse.  It gives the same
// answer for `set` and cannot give one at all for the three calls this
// package exists for.
//
//   * `insert` has to decide what whitespace and what newline come WITH
//     the new entry, and there is no span to patch because the entry
//     was never there.  A policy has to be consulted, and a design with
//     no item model has nowhere to consult one from.
//   * `rename` moves a key and leaves the value, its decor and its
//     trailing comment exactly where they were.  Over raw text that is
//     two splices whose offsets depend on each other.
//   * a `[dependencies]` table that does not exist yet has to be
//     created in the right PLACE — after the last table of the file,
//     not in the middle of `[package]` — and "the right place" is a
//     statement about the item tree.
//
// So the model is a tree of items, each carrying its decor, and the
// text is what the tree renders to rather than what it is.
//
// WHAT AN ITEM IS.  TOML has three kinds of thing that can hold other
// things and they are not interchangeable, so they are three variants
// rather than one with a flag: a standard table introduced by a
// `[header]`, an inline table written `{ a = 1 }`, and an array of
// tables written `[[header]]`.  Turning one into another changes the
// file's meaning, so nothing here does it silently — `retype` is the
// one call that does, it takes a policy, and it answers a `Result`.
//
// WHAT A VALUE IS.  A scalar keeps its REPR — the bytes it was
// written as — beside its decoded meaning, because `0x2A`, `42` and
// `4_2` are one integer and three files.  A caller that reads
// `int_of` gets 42; a caller that renders gets its own bytes back.
// `toml_edit`'s `Formatted<T>` is the same arrangement and for the
// same reason.

use teditkey

// Where a piece of a rendered document came from.
//
// The distinction a caller asserts on.  `TeditOriginal` means these
// exact bytes were in the input; `TeditProduced` means this package
// wrote them, under a `TeditPolicy` the caller chose.
pub enum TeditOrigin
    // Copied out of the parsed text, byte for byte.
    TeditOriginal
    // Written by this package.  Everything an edit added, and nothing
    // else, is this.
    TeditProduced

// A run of bytes in a document: either a range of the original text or
// text this editor made.
//
// THE LOAD-BEARING TYPE.  Two variants, no third, and every byte a
// render emits comes out of one of them.
//
// A span rather than a copied `Str` for the original half, so a
// document of any size costs one string and a list of integer pairs —
// and so that `origin_of` can answer without comparing text.
pub enum TeditRaw
    // Bytes `[start, end)` of the document's original text, verbatim.
    // Half-open, so an empty run at a position is expressible — which
    // is what a key with no decor in front of it has.
    TeditSpan(start: Int, end: Int)
    // Bytes this editor produced.  Nothing in a freshly parsed
    // document is one of these; every one in a rendered document is a
    // place the caller asked for a change.
    TeditMade(text: Str)

// The whitespace and comments around an item, kept so that rendering
// is the identity.
//
// `toml_edit` calls this a decor and the name is worth keeping: it is
// everything that is between the meaningful tokens, and losing it is
// exactly what makes a rewritten config file unreadable.
//
// `prefix` is what came before the item and after the previous one —
// the blank lines above a table header, the indentation before a key,
// the comment on its own line that documents the entry below it.
// `suffix` is what came after the item on the same line — the spaces
// before a trailing comment, and the comment.
//
// A NEWLINE BELONGS TO THE PREFIX OF WHAT FOLLOWS, not to the suffix
// of what precedes.  The rule is arbitrary and stating it is not: with
// the other convention, deleting the last key of a table takes the
// blank line that separated the table from the next one with it, and
// the file closes up by one line every time a tool runs.
pub struct TeditDecor
    prefix: TeditRaw
    suffix: TeditRaw

// What kind of thing an item is.
//
// A reader dispatches on this before asking for a payload, so the four
// accessors below (`value_of`, `table_of`, `array_of`, `pairs_of`) each
// have one kind they answer for and `None` for the rest.
pub enum TeditItemKind
    // A key with a value: `port = 8080`.
    TeditKeyValue
    // A table introduced by a header: `[server]`.
    TeditStandardTable
    // A table written between braces: `server = { port = 8080 }`.
    TeditInlineTable
    // One element of an array of tables: `[[bin]]`.
    TeditArrayTable
    // The implicit table a dotted header creates.  `[a.b.c]` declares
    // `a` and `a.b` without either appearing in the file, and an
    // implicit table renders as nothing at all — which is why it is a
    // kind rather than an empty standard table.
    TeditImplicitTable

// What kind of value a scalar or container is, under TOML's own names.
//
// `TeditValueKind` and `tomlnode.TomlType` answer the same question
// about the same data and are deliberately two types: this one
// distinguishes the two table SPELLINGS, which toml-nv's correctly does
// not, because to a value tree they are the same table.
pub enum TeditValueKind
    TeditStr
    TeditInt
    TeditFloat
    TeditBool
    TeditOffsetDateTime
    TeditLocalDateTime
    TeditLocalDate
    TeditLocalTime
    TeditArray
    TeditInlineTableValue

// One entry of a document: a key, what it holds, and the bytes around
// both.
//
// `key` is absent for the elements of an array — an array has
// positions and not names — and present for everything else.
//
// The children are held by INDEX into the document's own item list
// rather than inline, because a key-value pair inside a table inside
// an array of tables is three levels of nesting and a tree of boxed
// structs would make every walk a chain of allocations.  `teditdoc` is
// what owns the list; this module is what one entry in it means.
pub struct TeditItem
    kind: TeditItemKind
    // The key as it was written, with its own quoting style, or an
    // empty key for an array element.  `teditkey.is_empty` is the
    // check.
    key: teditkey.TeditKey
    // The bytes around the item.
    decor: TeditDecor
    // For a key-value pair: the value.  For a table: the header line.
    // For an implicit table: an empty `TeditSpan`.
    repr: TeditRaw
    // What the value is, when the item holds one.
    value_kind: TeditValueKind
    // Indices into the document's item list, in document order.  Empty
    // for a key-value pair whose value is a scalar.
    children: [Int]

// A value under construction, before it is put into a document.
//
// `insert` and `set` take one of these rather than a `TeditItem`,
// because a caller building a new entry has no decor to supply and no
// place in the item list to name — both are the document's business and
// the policy's.
pub struct TeditNewValue
    kind: TeditValueKind
    // The bytes to write.  A caller that wants control over the exact
    // spelling — `0x2A` rather than `42`, a literal string rather than
    // a basic one — writes them here, and the constructors below are
    // the shorthand for the ordinary case.
    repr: Str
    // The children, for an array or an inline table.  Empty otherwise.
    items: [TeditNewValue]
    // The keys of `items`, for an inline table.  Empty for an array.
    keys: [Str]

// ── Reading a raw ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The bytes a raw stands for, against the document text it came from.
//
// `source` is the ORIGINAL text of the document the raw was read out
// of.  Passing a different string answers a different, wrong run of
// bytes, which is why `teditdoc.raw_str` exists and takes the document
// instead — this is the primitive under it, published because a caller
// walking a document's items already holds the source.
//
// ```novo
// use tedititem
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let r = TeditSpan(start: 0, end: 4)
//     println(tedititem.raw_text(r, "port = 8080"))
// ```
pub fn raw_text(r: TeditRaw, source: Str) -> Str []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.raw_text")

// How many bytes a raw renders to.
//
// Answers without building the string, so sizing a buffer for
// `teditdoc.render_into` costs a walk and no allocation.
pub fn raw_len(r: TeditRaw, source: Str) -> Int []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.raw_len")

// Whether a raw is bytes of the original or bytes this package made.
//
// The assertion the package is designed around.  A render whose every
// raw answers `TeditOriginal` is byte-identical to its input, and one
// with three `TeditProduced` raws changed exactly three runs.
pub fn origin_of(r: TeditRaw) -> TeditOrigin []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.origin_of")

// Whether a raw stands for no bytes at all.
//
// True for `TeditSpan(n, n)` and for `TeditMade("")`, which are the
// same rendered output and two different statements about where it
// came from — so this is a question about LENGTH and `origin_of` is
// the question about provenance.
pub fn raw_empty(r: TeditRaw, source: Str) -> Bool []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.raw_empty")

// A raw for text this package made.
//
// Every constructor below routes through this one, so there is a single
// place where produced bytes enter a document.
pub fn made(text: Str) -> TeditRaw []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.made")

// ── Reading an item ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The decor around an item.
pub fn decor_of(it: TeditItem) -> TeditDecor []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.decor_of")

// An item with different decor, and everything else untouched.
//
// The call a caller makes to move a comment or change indentation
// without touching the value — which is the edit a formatter makes and
// the edit `set` must not make.
pub fn with_decor(it: TeditItem, d: TeditDecor) -> TeditItem []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.with_decor")

// A decor of no bytes at all, on both sides.
//
// What an item inserted into an empty document starts with before a
// policy is applied to it.
pub fn empty_decor() -> TeditDecor []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.empty_decor")

// The comment carried in a decor's prefix, without its `#` and without
// the newline, or the empty string when there is none.
//
// A prefix can hold several comment lines and any amount of blank
// space; this answers them joined by newlines, which is what a caller
// showing a key's documentation wants.  `prefix_comment_lines` is the
// same thing unjoined.
pub fn prefix_comment(d: TeditDecor, source: Str) -> Str []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.prefix_comment")

// Each comment line in a decor's prefix, in order, `#` stripped.
pub fn prefix_comment_lines(d: TeditDecor, source: Str) -> [Str] []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.prefix_comment_lines")

// The trailing comment on an item's own line, `#` stripped, or the
// empty string when there is none.
pub fn suffix_comment(d: TeditDecor, source: Str) -> Str []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.suffix_comment")

// ── Reading a value ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

// What kind of value an item holds.
pub fn kind_of(it: TeditItem) -> TeditValueKind []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.kind_of")

// TOML's own name for a value kind: `string`, `integer`, `float`,
// `boolean`, `offset date-time`, `local date-time`, `local date`,
// `local time`, `array`, `table`.
//
// The spelling the specification uses, so a diagnostic that names a
// type names the one a reader will find in it.
pub fn kind_name(k: TeditValueKind) -> Str []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.kind_name")

// The decoded string of a string item, escapes resolved, or `None`
// when the item is not a string.
//
// DECODED, which is the whole difference between this and `repr`.
// `path = "C:\\tmp"` reads back as `C:\tmp` here and as `"C:\\tmp"`
// from `repr_of`, and a caller writing the value into another file
// wants the first while a caller rendering wants the second.
pub fn str_of(it: TeditItem, source: Str) -> ?Str []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.str_of")

// The value of an integer item, or `None` when the item is not one.
//
// Decodes the four bases TOML admits and strips underscores, so
// `0x2A`, `0o52`, `0b101010` and `4_2` all answer 42 — and all four
// render back as themselves.
pub fn int_of(it: TeditItem, source: Str) -> ?Int []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.int_of")

// The value of a float item, or `None` when the item is not one.
//
// `inf`, `-inf` and `nan` are floats in TOML and are answered as such.
pub fn float_of(it: TeditItem, source: Str) -> ?Float []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.float_of")

// The value of a boolean item, or `None` when the item is not one.
pub fn bool_of(it: TeditItem, source: Str) -> ?Bool []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.bool_of")

// The bytes an item's value was written as, verbatim.
//
// The other half of the pair with `str_of` and friends: this is what
// renders, that is what the value means.
pub fn repr_of(it: TeditItem, source: Str) -> Str []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.repr_of")

// Whether an item's value is still the bytes it was parsed from.
//
// False after any `set` that touched it, true for every item of a
// freshly parsed document — so a caller can ask which entries a run of
// edits actually moved without diffing text.
pub fn value_untouched(it: TeditItem) -> Bool []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.value_untouched")

// ── Building a new value ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The constructors a caller hands to `teditdoc.set` and
// `teditdoc.insert`.  Each one produces the ORDINARY spelling; a caller
// who wants another writes `TeditNewValue` itself with its own `repr`,
// which is why the field is public.

// A string value, quoted and escaped under the policy's string style.
//
// The escaping is the policy's and not this call's, because a document
// written with literal strings should keep getting them — see
// `teditfmt.TeditStringStyle`.
//
// ```novo
// use tedititem
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let v = tedititem.string_value("^0.0.3")
//     println(v.repr)
// ```
pub fn string_value(text: Str) -> TeditNewValue []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.string_value")

// An integer value, in decimal.
pub fn int_value(n: Int) -> TeditNewValue []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.int_value")

// An integer value written in a base: 2, 8, 10 or 16.
//
// Any other base is decimal, because TOML has four and inventing a
// fifth would produce a file no parser reads.
pub fn int_value_base(n: Int, base: Int) -> TeditNewValue []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.int_value_base")

// A float value.
//
// The shortest decimal that reads back as the same double, which is
// what every other TOML writer does and what keeps a round trip from
// growing digits.
pub fn float_value(f: Float) -> TeditNewValue []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.float_value")

// A boolean value: `true` or `false`, which are TOML's only spellings.
pub fn bool_value(b: Bool) -> TeditNewValue []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.bool_value")

// An array value, rendered inline under the policy.
//
// An array of arrays is this call nested, and an array long enough to
// want one element per line is the policy's `wrap_arrays_over`
// deciding, not this call's.
pub fn array_value(items: [TeditNewValue]) -> TeditNewValue []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.array_value")

// An inline table value: `{ a = 1, b = 2 }`.
//
// `keys` and `values` are positional pairs and must be the same
// length; a mismatch is a refusal from `teditdoc.set`, not from here,
// because a constructor that answered a `Result` could not be nested
// inside another constructor without unwrapping at every level.
pub fn inline_table_value(keys: [Str], values: [TeditNewValue]) -> TeditNewValue []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.inline_table_value")

// A value written exactly as the caller spells it.
//
// The escape hatch, and the reason the package does not need a
// constructor per exotic spelling: a caller who wants `1979-05-27T07:32:00Z`
// or a multi-line basic string writes the bytes and says what kind they
// are.  The bytes are NOT validated here — `teditdoc.set` parses them
// back and refuses what does not read as the declared kind, so a typo
// is caught at the edit rather than at the next reader.
pub fn raw_value(kind: TeditValueKind, repr: Str) -> TeditNewValue []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.raw_value")

// Whether a new value's spelling parses as the kind it claims.
//
// What `teditdoc.set` runs over a `raw_value` before it accepts it,
// published so a caller can ask first.
pub fn new_value_ok(v: TeditNewValue) -> Bool []
    todo("toml-edit-nv.tedititem.new_value_ok")
